.TH CLOG_SET_FIELD 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_set_field \- attach or update a persistent structured field
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_set_field(clog logger, const char *key, const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Attaches key with value as a persistent field.  The field then appears
in every later log line that this logger writes.  It sits between the
func= and msg= tokens:

  ts=... level=INFO proc=prog(pid):tname(tid) src=... func=... key=value msg=...

The library copies both key and value internally.  The caller can free
them or change them after this call, and the logger does not notice.

A key that already exists gets the new value.  Otherwise the field set
takes a new field.

Every character of a key must be a printable US-ASCII character
(0x21-0x7e).  The characters '=', ']', '"' and '\e\e' are not allowed.
The library silently ignores a key that is empty.  It also ignores a
key that holds one of those forbidden characters, whitespace, an ASCII
control character, or any byte that is not ASCII.  Every log line
already carries a set of fixed tokens.  They are ts, level, proc, src,
func, msg, bt and bt_error.  The library ignores a key that names one
of them too.  Such a key would make a duplicate key= token in logfmt,
a duplicate JSON key, or a duplicate RFC 5424 SD-PARAM-NAME inside one
SD-ELEMENT.

A value can hold a space, an '=', a '"', a '\e\e' or an ASCII control
character.  The library then double-quotes that value in the output,
and it escapes those characters with a backslash:
  - '"' -> \e"
  - '\e\e' -> \e\e
  - control chars -> \en, \er, \et, or \exNN

The exact syntax of an escape differs by output format.  See
clog_set_format(3).  JSON uses \euNNNN Unicode escapes.  RFC 5424
syslog escapes a '"', a '\e\e' and a ']', as well as a control
character.
When the output format is CLOG_FMT_JSON or CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG, a value can
hold text that is not well-formed UTF-8.  The library replaces each
invalid byte with the Unicode replacement character (U+FFFD), and it
does not embed the byte raw.  JSON text must be valid Unicode, and RFC
5424 requires an SD-PARAM-VALUE to be UTF-8.
An RFC 5424 SD-PARAM-NAME longer than 32 characters becomes its first
23 characters, then a '~', and then a hash of the full key in 8 hex
digits.  A bare prefix of 32 characters would not do.  Two different
long keys that share a prefix therefore still get different
SD-PARAM-NAMEs.
In every format, the library always neutralizes a control character.
That covers a literal newline and a carriage return.  A field value can
therefore never split one record across several lines.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
key     Field name. Copied internally. Silently ignored if empty,
        invalid, or one of the reserved names listed above.
value   Field value. Copied internally. May be arbitrary text.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Thread-safe: protected by the per-logger mutex.
Every emitted line visits each attached field once; keep the field
count small if logging is a hot path.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_remove_field (3),
.BR clog_clear_fields (3)
